  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-PSE130</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of tripartite constituents to revise the labour law in line with international labour standards</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Labour Law Reform is a key element of the Decent Work Country Program for Palestine, which underscores improved labour market governance as a key area of cooperation between the ILO and Palestinian tripartite constituents. 
The Ministry of Labour led tripartite dialogues on amending the Labour Law, aiming to reconcile it with proposed revisions and the draft Social Security Law based on a gap analysis provided by the ILO. Significant progress was made; which unfortunately came to an end due to escalating conflicts. The proposed amendments included revisions to eight articles in the Labour Law of 2000 critical to achieving legislative harmony between the Palestinian Labour Law and the draft Social Security Law. Though there has been a pause, there is hope that these important discussions will resume, with an emphasis on reducing the negative impacts of the conflict for both businesses and employees. 
Moving forward, the ILO will engage in new activities to support the MoL and social partners through working with the Tripartite National Committee on Labour Law Reform, following a review of draft labour legislation. This review will include providing comments and recommendations for amendments to regulatory texts for the labour law, emphasizing gender equality and non-discrimination.  
As part of the UN country team, the ILO will continue to advocate for the inclusion of specific outputs related to labour legislation reforms within the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UNSDCF), to ensure coherent country programming in line with ILS.</narrative>
  </description>
